{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Functional Requirements: Use Case and Use Story"}, {"type": "position", "content": 3}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear and use when you are discussing about important aspects within functional requirements. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Make the coding of the system modules and the main program, starting from using the chosen programming language, based on design specifications.   Exchanges information about requirements types and their characteristics."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.   Exchanges simple information on the graphic layout of an IT solution, functional requirements of a business through the IT solution, and the functional phase of an IT project."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob03re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Future With the Present Simple"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>The <strong>simple present</strong> tense does not always refer to the present time. You can also use it to talk about the future.</p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Professor Gonzalez, who is a prominent figure in the field of programming, visits us this afternoon."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Now, we have to hurry up, Mr. Gonzalez' conference starts in 30 minutes"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>The <strong>simple present</strong> tense can also have a future connotation as it can refer to events taking place in the near <strong>future</strong>. In order to change its connotation of time, we have to add a time expression right next to the sentence we write in <strong>simple present</strong>. As you are referring to specific events in a specific moment in the <strong>future</strong>, the time expressions that you use have to be as precise as possible; thus, expressions like <i>tonight at 9 o&rsquo;clock</i>, <i>in half an hour</i>, or <i>tomorrow at 6 pm</i> are needed.</p> <p>Structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + <strong>verb</strong> (<strong>present simple</strong>) + time expression (precise in the future).</p> <p style=\"text-align: left;\">Examples:</p> <p><i>The meeting to check the business goals <strong>takes</strong> place <strong>next Monday</strong>.</i></p> <p><i>The supervisors <strong>meet</strong>&nbsp;<strong>tonight</strong>. </i></p>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Functional Requirements: In a Use Case and an User Story"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Functional requirements must contain two very important factors in order to achieve success, use cases and user stories."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p><strong>USE CASE </strong></p> <p>A use case is basically a list of a series of actions that describe the interactions and relationship between the developed system and a role, which is basically the actor (human user or other systems) in the achievement of a goal.&nbsp; The way these are written are by sequence steps or flows following the format <i>(Actor/System)</i> does<i> (action)</i>.</p> <p><strong>USER STORY</strong></p> <p>The user stories show in a short but detailed description of the different uses of the developed system. These explanations are done step-by-step and are meant to guide and teach the user how to use the system. The language that is used in user stories is simple and basic so any user can understand; however, it tends to follow a specific model: <i>As a (human user or other systems) I want (specific action from the system) so that (final achievement). &nbsp;&nbsp;</i></p>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "prominent figure"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a very knowledgeable person; an expert on a specific field"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Professor Gonzalez, who is a prominent figure in the field of programming, visits us this afternoon."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob03aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "smoothly"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "without any trouble or interruption"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: All the following steps that are coming after the requirement is written will flow smoothly."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob03aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "misinterpretation"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a wrong and incorrect understanding of a topic, situation, or thing"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There will be no place for misinterpretations."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob03aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "achievable"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "possible to achieve or accomplish"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The disadvantages of functional requirements will show up only when they are not necessary, concise, achievable, unambiguous, complete nor verifiable."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob03aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "impede"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to not allow something to take place or happen"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They will impede the requirement to convert into a fully operational system or application. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob03aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "shoot"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an exclamation form to express annoyance"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Shoot, I don't know what I am going to ask him now."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob03aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for further information about use case and user story in functional requirements."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make notes about the main features in each one of them"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Design a poster in which you show the main similarities and differences."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share with your tutor and answer all his questions."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for information about use case and user story in functional requirements."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I design a poster about use case and in a user story features."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I talk about similarities and differences."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n5le02ob03re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n5le02ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p><strong>Margarita</strong>: Professor Gonzalez, who is a prominent figure in the field of programming, visits us this afternoon. He will discuss important aspects of the functional requirements, and I&rsquo;m going to prepare as many questions as possible. I hope he addresses the topic of functional requirements today.</p>\n<p><strong>Carlos</strong>: I think I will ask him about the advantages and disadvantages of the functional requirements.</p>"}, {"time": "21000", "image": "enpe8n5le02ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p><strong>Margarita</strong>: You don&rsquo;t have to ask Mr. Gonzalez! I can give you the answer right now. On the one hand, the advantages of functional requirements are really easy to understand. When you write a requirement that has good syntax and provides the complete and concise idea of what you want the system to perform, it is going to be reflected on the final product and the way it is achieved. All the following steps that are coming after the requirement is written will flow smoothly, and there will be no place for misinterpretations. On the other hand, the disadvantages of functional requirements will show up only when they are not necessary, concise, achievable, unambiguous, complete nor verifiable. They will impede the requirement to convert into a fully operational system or application.</p>"}, {"time": "66000", "image": "enpe8n5le02ob02re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p><strong>Carlos</strong>: Wow. I&rsquo;ll ask him how to use case and user stories look like, then.</p>\n<p><strong>Margarita</strong>: Well, you don&rsquo;t have to ask that either. The functional requirements in use cases look like a series of different actions that describe the steps of the interactions between the system and the role. Now, the user stories portray a detailed description of the uses of the system which will guide and teach the user how to use it.</p>\n<p><strong>Carlos</strong>: Shoot, I don&rsquo;t know what I am going to ask him now.</p>\n<p><strong>Margarita</strong>: Think about that later. Now, we have to hurry up, Mr. Gonzalez&rsquo; conference starts in 30 minutes.</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n5le02ob03re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": 